1.3. Finer control over pinning with freezeTime

In the above examples, setting the freezeTime meant that the following visits were pinned:

All visits that had already been completed or started.

All visits that technicians were already traveling to.

While pinning the visits that technicians were already traveling to is a safe option in terms of not disrupting the technicians' schedules, it may not always be necessary.

Imagine a scenario where Ann just finished Visit A at 09:00 and her next visit (Visit B) starts at 14:00. It will take Ann 1 hour to travel to Visit B, so she has plenty of time.

If the planning update happens at 11:00 (freezeTime is set to 11:00), Visit B would be pinned because Ann is already traveling to it, even though she might have enough time to handle an emergency visit at 12:00:

{
  "modelInput": {
    "freezeTime": "2027-02-01T11:00:00Z"
  }
}
JSON
Copy

In order to avoid automatic pinning of visits that technicians are already traveling to, you can set the pinNextVisitDuringFreeze property to NEVER. This property can be set globally in the modelInput section of the route plan input, or for a specific vehicle shift (if both are set, the setting for the vehicle shift takes precedence):

{
  "modelInput": {
    "freezeTime": "2027-02-01T12:00:00Z",
    "pinNextVisitDuringFreeze": "ALWAYS",
    "vehicles": [
      {
        "id": "Ann",
        "shifts": [
          {
            "id": "Ann-2027-02-01",
            "pinNextVisitDuringFreeze": "NEVER"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
JSON
Copy

With this setting, you can use Visit.pinningRequested to pin specific visits that must not be rescheduled according to your needs:

{
  "modelInput": {
    "freezeTime": "2027-02-01T12:00:00Z",
    "pinNextVisitDuringFreeze": "NEVER",
    "visits": [
      {
        "id": "Visit B",
        "minStartTravelTime": "2027-02-01T00:00:00Z",
        "pinningRequested": true
      }
    ]
  }
}
JSON
Copy

Instead of resubmitting the full updated dataset, you can make this change with the Patch feature, which submits only the change as a JSON Patch document and generates a new revision of the plan. The Patch feature is currently available as a preview feature. If you’d like early access to it, please contact us.

The freeze at 11:00 shown above is set with:

{
  "op": "add",
  "path": "/freezeTime",
  "value": "2027-02-01T11:00:00Z"
}
JSON
Copy

Setting pinNextVisitDuringFreeze to ALWAYS globally, with an override for Ann’s shift, is set with:

[
  {
    "op": "add",
    "path": "/freezeTime",
    "value": "2027-02-01T12:00:00Z"
  },
  {
    "op": "add",
    "path": "/pinNextVisitDuringFreeze",
    "value": "ALWAYS"
  },
  {
    "op": "add",
    "path": "/vehicles/[id=Ann]/shifts/[id=Ann-2027-02-01]/pinNextVisitDuringFreeze",
    "value": "NEVER"
  }
]
JSON
Copy

Pinning Visit B individually with Visit.pinningRequested is set with:

[
  {
    "op": "add",
    "path": "/freezeTime",
    "value": "2027-02-01T12:00:00Z"
  },
  {
    "op": "add",
    "path": "/pinNextVisitDuringFreeze",
    "value": "NEVER"
  },
  {
    "op": "add",
    "path": "/visits/[id=Visit B]/pinningRequested",
    "value": true
  },
  {
    "op": "add",
    "path": "/visits/[id=Visit B]/minStartTravelTime",
    "value": "2027-02-01T00:00:00Z"
  }
]
JSON
Copy

See /from-patch for more information about the /from-patch/ endpoint and the patch payload format.

To summarize, the pinNextVisitDuringFreeze property values have the following meaning:

ALWAYS (default): All visits that are completed, started or that technicians might already be traveling to before the freezeTime are pinned.

NEVER: All visits that are completed or started before the freezeTime are pinned.

2. Block pinning whole vehicle shifts

In some cases, it may be useful to pin the whole vehicle shift itinerary, for instance, if the vehicle shift already ended (imagine a multi-day schedule).

Field service routing will automatically pin all vehicle shifts that have maxEndTime or maxLastVisitDepartureTime before or equal to the freezeTime.

Alternatively, pinning the whole vehicle shift can be used as a convenient shortcut to pin all visits in the vehicle shift itinerary. Note that pinning the whole vehicle shift means also that no new visits can be added to the vehicle shift itinerary (as opposed to pinning just the individual visits).

You can pin the whole vehicle shift by setting pinned to true for the vehicle shift:

{
  "modelInput": {
    "vehicles": [
      {
        "id": "Ann",
        "shifts": [
          {
            "id": "Ann-2027-02-01",
            "pinned": true
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
JSON
Copy

Instead of resubmitting the full updated dataset, you can make this change with the Patch feature, which submits only the change as a JSON Patch document and generates a new revision of the plan. The Patch feature is currently available as a preview feature. If you’d like early access to it, please contact us.

[
  {
    "op": "add",
    "path": "/vehicles/[id=Ann]/shifts/[id=Ann-2027-02-01]/pinned",
    "value": true
  }
]
JSON
Copy

See /from-patch for more information about the /from-patch/ endpoint and the patch payload format.

Vehicle shift pinning can be combined with visit pinning and pinning by freezeTime.

3. Block pinning partial vehicle shifts

In some cases, even batch planning may need to take into account existing visit assignments (pinned visits) without considering all parts of the plan as a history that cannot be modified.

For instance, if visits for only one technician before a specific time needs to be pinned, but other visits being attended by other technicians do not need to be pinned.

You can pin specific visits without setting the freezeTime. This will result in only the specific visits that have been pinned being frozen in the plan.

This scenario also requires that if there is a pinned visit C assigned to a vehicle shift, all visits in this vehicle shift’s itinerary before C must be pinned as well.

For a schedule with visits A, B, C, and D assigned to the same vehicle shift, if Visit C needs to be pinned, then Visit A and Visit B must also be pinned.

4. Pinning individual visits

To avoid the restriction that the preceding visits of a pinned visit have to be pinned as well, you can pin visits on a vehicle shift itinerary to a certain startServiceTime.

This can be achieved by setting startServiceTime as well as setting pin to true for an item on a vehicle shift itinerary. As a result, the solver will not modify the start service time for a visit pinned that way. It might, however, modify the itinerary before and after the visit (and not only after the pinned visit, as with the pinningRequested flag).

The Keep a pinned visit’s start service time hard constraint is invoked when a visit has been pinned to a specific start time but the solver schedules it to start at a different time. The constraint adds a hard penalty to the dataset score that is calculated by multiplying the number of seconds between the pinned start time and the actual scheduled start time by 100,000.

The Keep a pinned visit’s vehicle shift hard constraint is invoked when a pinned visit is assigned to a different vehicle shift than the one it was pinned to. The constraint adds a hard penalty of 10,000,000,000 to the dataset score for each visit assigned to the wrong shift, ensuring Timefold to keep pinned visits on their designated vehicle shift.

Setting pin to true requires startServiceTime to also be set.

{
  "modelInput": {
    "vehicles": [
      {
        "id": "Ann",
        "shifts": [
          {
            "id": "Ann-2027-02-01",
            "itinerary": [
              {
                "id": "Visit A",
                "kind": "VISIT",
                "startServiceTime": "2027-02-01T11:25:19Z",
                "pin": true
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
JSON
Copy

Instead of resubmitting the full updated dataset, you can make this change with the Patch feature, which submits only the change as a JSON Patch document and generates a new revision of the plan. The Patch feature is currently available as a preview feature. If you’d like early access to it, please contact us.

